# The canonical harness run: browse a tree, search it, move something, take it back.
# Everything here happens in a window the user never sees, against a throwaway tree the
# run makes for itself.

tree .
refresh
assert-count 8
assert-row Documents
shot 01-browse

# Folder-local search: recursive, flattened, and each hit shows the folder it came from.
# Three report.md files, one per level, is what the fixture is for.
search report
assert-flattened
assert-count 3
assert-status result
shot 02-search
clear-search
assert-not-flattened

# A move and its undo. Both go through TransferPlanner/TransferExecutor — the same code
# a drag-and-drop does — so a rule mutated in either shows up here.
select photo.jpg, archive.zip
assert-selected 2
shot 03-selected
move to Documents
assert-no-row photo.jpg
assert-exists Documents/photo.jpg
assert-can-undo
undo
assert-row photo.jpg
assert-missing Documents/photo.jpg

# A rename, numbered across a selection: "Holiday" over two files gives Holiday 1/2,
# each keeping its own extension.
select notes.txt, report.md
rename Holiday
assert-row "Holiday 1.txt"
assert-row "Holiday 2.md"
undo
assert-row notes.txt

# Creating, which is additive: it appears, it is selected, and it leaves Ctrl+Z pointing at
# whatever came before rather than spending the slot. tools/ui/newitem.bbs covers the rest.
newfolder Reports
settle
assert-row Reports
assert-selected 1

# The real Recycle Bin, and Ctrl+Z putting it back out again.
select budget.xlsx
delete
assert-missing budget.xlsx
undo
assert-exists budget.xlsx

# The byte-level transfer surface, posed rather than run: a real transfer fast enough to be safe
# here is over before a capture could catch it, and a slow one would put a different throughput
# figure and time remaining into every picture. The numbers are given; the strip is the app's own.
progress-demo
assert-transfer "112 MB/s"
assert-transfer "of 50.000 GB"
assert-transfer "photo.jpg"
shot 05-transfer-progress
dialog transfer 06-transfer-details

# The degraded shape: a plan the size index could not total. Bytes and throughput, but no
# percentage and no time left — an indeterminate bar, never one stuck at zero.
progress-demo unsized
assert-transfer "112 MB/s"
assert-transfer-indeterminate
shot 07-transfer-unsized
progress-demo off

# Tabs, panes and the tile view — the layout a capture is usually about.
newtab Documents
assert-tabs 2
split right Pictures
assert-panes 2
thumbnails 0.5
shot 04-panes

# The preview pane appears and goes away. What it can actually show is preview.bbs's job — this
# is here so the canonical pass notices if it stops appearing at all, and it deliberately asserts
# nothing about content: the fixture's files carry the right extensions and the wrong bytes.
preview on
assert-visible PreviewPane
preview off
assert-hidden PreviewPane

# Nothing up to here may have tried to put another program's window on screen.
assert-not-launched

# Last, because it is the one gesture in this script that *should* reach the launcher.
#
# The fixture's archive.zip is 512,000 bytes of filler, and this list has walked past it since
# long before archives were browsable. It has to stay walked past: those bytes are all zeroes,
# which is a *valid empty tar*, so an archive layer that trusted detection over the suffix would
# quietly take the tab inside it and show an empty folder. Entering it must hand the file back to
# whatever owns the extension — which this run refuses, and says so.
go .
enter archive.zip
assert-row archive.zip
assert-not-inside-archive
assert-status was not launched

state
